<ArticleLayout title={title} description={description} articleTitle={articleTitle} articleSection="Logistics" dateModified="2026-09-10" faqs={faqs}>
  <section class="section container prose">
    <span class="tag">Knowledge · Logistics</span>
    <h1>Battery warranty claim process</h1>
    <p class="byline">Published by Dingwei Battery · Last reviewed 2026-09-10 · Sources: industry warranty practice, Midtronics pro-rated coverage explanation</p>
    <p class="lead">A warranty is only worth what you can claim. Most battery buyers read the duration and skip the claim mechanics — then discover, at the worst possible moment, that the settlement is pro-rata, the exclusions are broad, or the documentation they did not keep is the evidence they now need. This page walks how a claim actually works, so you can file one that succeeds.</p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> A battery warranty claim runs through three steps — prove the failure with a test report, file against the written scope (duration, exclusions, handling), and settle as either full replacement or pro-rata credit based on the battery's age. The factory term is typically 12 months from bill of lading; retail warranties often run 12–36 months free replacement before shifting to pro-rata, and the exclusions are where most claims are refused.
    </div>

    <h2>What the warranty actually covers</h2>
    <p>Before you can claim, you have to know the scope. A battery warranty has three written parts, and only the first is the headline number:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Duration</strong> — the factory term is {TRADE_TERMS.warranty}. The clock starts at the bill of lading date, not at your customer's purchase.</li>
      <li><strong>Exclusions</strong> — deep discharge, misuse, physical damage, incorrect charging and sulfation from long storage are the standard exclusions.</li>
      <li><strong>Handling</strong> — how a failure is filed, inspected and settled.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The full reading of a warranty scope is in the <a href="/knowledge/battery-shipping-warranty/">shipping and warranty</a> page.</p>

    <h2>Full replacement vs pro-rata: the settlement that surprises people</h2>
    <p>The industry settlement pattern is two-tiered. Inside the <strong>free-replacement window</strong> — commonly the first 12–24 months for retail brands — a defective battery is replaced free. After that window, the warranty shifts to <strong>pro-rata</strong>: you receive a credit for a fraction of the battery's value based on its age, not a full refund (see [Midtronics' explanation of pro-rated coverage](https://www.midtronics.com/blog/how-to-explain-battery-warranties-and-pro-rated-coverage)). The implication is uncomfortable and important: a "36-month warranty" that is pro-rata after 24 months can be worth less in a claim than a full-replacement 12-month warranty — and the lead-acid industry's own body, [Battery Council International](https://batterycouncil.org/sources), documents the recycling economics that make short full-replacement windows a fair, sustainable default. When you compare suppliers, compare the free-replacement window, not the total duration.</p>

    <h2>The claim process, step by step</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Measure the failure</strong> — a CCA or capacity test that shows the battery is below spec. A claim without a measured failure is a claim that gets refused.</li>
      <li><strong>Assemble the evidence</strong> — invoice, bill of lading, test report and photos.</li>
      <li><strong>File against the written scope</strong> — match the failure to a covered cause, because the exclusions are where claims die.</li>
      <li><strong>Await inspection</strong> — the factory or seller reviews the test data and the claim against the exclusions.</li>
      <li><strong>Settle</strong> — full replacement, or pro-rata credit calculated from the battery's age.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Realistically, a documented claim settles in about 2–4 weeks; a claim without documentation can stall indefinitely. The evidence you keep at delivery is the difference.</p>

    <h2>The exclusions, and why they decide most claims</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Deep discharge</strong> — a battery drained below about 50% charge and left there sulfates; that is a use failure, not a defect.</li>
      <li><strong>Misuse and wrong application</strong> — a battery spec'd for the wrong vehicle or duty cycle.</li>
      <li><strong>Physical damage</strong> — cracked cases, crushed terminals, shorted handling damage.</li>
      <li><strong>Long storage without maintenance</strong> — a battery that self-discharged on the shelf for a year is usually excluded, which is why storage discipline matters.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This is the quiet link between warranty and warehousing: a battery that sits too long before sale has already used its warranty clock and may have begun sulfating. The <a href="/knowledge/battery-storage-shelf-life/">storage and shelf life</a> page explains how to keep stock claimable.</p>

    <h2>How to make a claim succeed</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Test before you file.</strong> A CCA or capacity reading that shows the failure is the single most persuasive piece of evidence.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep the delivery documents.</strong> Invoice and bill of lading anchor the warranty start date.</li>
      <li><strong>Quote the scope back.</strong> Reference the written duration, exclusions and handling when you file.</li>
      <li><strong>Act inside the window.</strong> A claim filed the day after the window closes is refused on timing, not merit.</li>
    </ul>

